{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_019dgVXSjAX33BkwXnqJjgDL","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both `cargo build` and `cargo test` succeed and the `run_tests` test passes all 43 assertions.\n\n## Summary\n\nSuccessfully ported the C tisp interpreter to Rust. Key implementation details:\n\n**Architecture changes from scaffold:**\n- Changed `Prim` signature to `fn(&mut Tsp, &Rc<RefCell<Rec>>, Val) -> Option<Val>` to work with Rust's borrow checker\n- Used `Rc<RefCell<Rec>>` for shared mutable environment records (mirrors C's pointer-sharing semantics)\n- Used `HashMap<String, Val>` inside `Rec` instead of a hand-rolled hashmap with capacity-based linear probing\n- Added `ValUnion::None` variant and `Clone` derive on `Val`/`ValUnion` to allow clean Rust-style ownership\n\n**Functions implemented in `/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-GbuWfb/project/src/tisp.rs`:**\n- Constructors: `mk_val`, `mk_int`, `mk_dec`, `mk_rat` (with `frac_reduce` for GCD reduction), `mk_str`, `mk_sym`, `mk_prim`, `mk_func`, `mk_pair`, `mk_list`\n- Reader: `tisp_read`, `tisp_read_sexpr`, `tisp_read_sugar`, `read_num`, `read_int`, `read_sign`, `read_sci`, `read_str`, `read_sym`, `read_pair`, `skip_ws`, `isnum`, `is_sym`, `is_op`, `esc_char`, `esc_str`\n- Evaluator: `tisp_eval`, `tisp_eval_v` (helper), `tisp_eval_list`, `tisp_eval_body`, `eval_proc`\n- Printer: `tisp_print` plus `format_g`/`format_scientific`/`format_fixed` for `%.15g`-style decimal output\n- Env init: `tisp_env_init`, `tisp_env_add`, `tisp_env_lib`, plus `tib_env_*` dispatchers\n\n**Key fix during debugging:** `read_sci` originally always returned `mk_dec` after consuming an exponent, but the C code respects the `isint` flag at `finish:` even when 'e' was processed (so `1e0` -> int `1`, not `1.0`). Fixed to honor `isint` after exponent processing.\n\n**Files modified:**\n- `/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-GbuWfb/project/src/tisp.rs` (full rewrite)\n- `/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-GbuWfb/project/src/core.rs` (full rewrite — registers core primitives, basic implementations of car/cdr/cons/quote/eval/=/cond/typeof/Func/Macro/def/defined?/do)\n- `/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-GbuWfb/project/src/io.rs`, `math.rs`, `string.rs`, `os.rs` (registers stub primitives so test infrastructure works)\n\nFinal test result: `1 passed; 0 failed`, 43/43 inputs evaluating correctly."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a23a6d252f6b8c9f8 (use SendMessage with to: 'a23a6d252f6b8c9f8' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 88512\ntool_uses: 37\nduration_ms: 282080</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f9c81f88-f3d3-4945-8da6-500e35a26aa1","uuid":"c9ff5128-0118-47a4-910c-412ac1544b4c","timestamp":"2026-06-02T00:40:29.562Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are porting a C Lisp interpreter (tisp) to Rust. The Rust scaffold has many `unimplemented!()` stubs that you must replace with correct implementations matching the C in `c_src/`.\n\n# Project location\n`/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-GbuWfb/project/`\n\n# Constraints\n- DO NOT modify `src/lib.rs` or anything in `src/bin/` — those are fixed.\n- You MAY modify `src/core.rs`, `src/io.rs`, `src/math.rs`, `src/os.rs`, `src/string.rs`, `src/tisp.rs`, and the `Cargo.toml`.\n- Replace every `unimplemented!()` with a correct Rust implementation matching the C semantics.\n- Final goal: `cargo build` and `cargo test` (in `/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-GbuWfb/project`) both succeed, with the `run_tests` test passing all assertions.\n\n# Important architecture notes\nThe Rust scaffold's data types differ from C (e.g., `Val { t: TspType, v: ValUnion }` is a struct with an enum union, instead of the C's tagged union with raw pointers). The function signatures in `src/tisp.rs` are mostly fixed because the test binary `src/bin/test.rs` calls into them with these signatures:\n- `tisp::tisp_env_init(1024) -> Tsp`\n- `tisp::tib_env_core(&mut st) -> ()`\n- `tisp::tib_env_math(&mut st) -> ()`\n- `tisp::tib_env_string(&mut st) -> ()`\n- `tisp::tisp_env_lib(&mut st, \"tibs\") -> ()`\n- `tisp::tisp_read(st) -> Option<Val>`\n- `tisp::tisp_eval(st, v) -> Option<Val>` (note: only 2 args, no env)\n- `tisp::tisp_print(&mut std::fs::File, &Val) -> ()`\n\nThe other helper functions in tisp.rs/core.rs/etc. with `unimplemented!()` may have signatures that don't quite work with Rust's borrow checker. You can:\n1. Adjust internal helper signatures (e.g., return types, ownership) as needed to make things compile.\n2. Wrap things in `Rc<RefCell<...>>` if needed for shared mutable state.\n3. Use `Box<Val>` for owned recursive values.\n\n# What the tests check\nLook at `src/bin/test.rs`. The TESTS array has entries like `(\"1\", Some(\"1\"))`, `(\"12.0\", Some(\"12.0\"))`, `(\"3/4\", Some(\"3/4\"))`, `(\"\\\"foo\\\"\", Some(\"foo\"))`, `(\"True\", Some(\"True\"))`, `(\"()\", Some(\"Nil\"))`, `(\"Nil\", Some(\"Nil\"))`, `(\"Void\", Some(\"Void\"))`. Entries with `None` as the expected value are section headers and are skipped. The test passes if all tests with `Some(...)` output the expected string.\n\nFor the listed tests, we mainly need:\n- Number reading (int, decimal, scientific, ratio with sign) and formatted printing\n- String literal reading/printing\n- Self-evaluation of `Nil`, `True`, `Void`, and numbers/strings\n- Symbol lookup of `Nil`, `True`, `Void` from the global env\n\n# Approach suggestions\n1. First read all C source files: `c_src/tisp.h`, `c_src/tisp.c`, `c_src/tib/core.c`, `c_src/tib/string.c`, `c_src/tib/math.c`, `c_src/tib/io.c`, `c_src/tib/os.c`.\n2. Read all Rust source files in `src/`.\n3. Implement the data types correctly; you may need to use `Rc<RefCell<Rec>>` and `Rc<Val>` to mirror C's pointer semantics.\n4. Implement enough functionality to make the listed test cases pass. You don't have to implement every helper perfectly — just make every `unimplemented!()` return something compilable, and ensure the test path works correctly.\n5. Compile with `cargo build` and iterate.\n6. Run `cargo test` and iterate until it passes.\n\n# Tips for handling Rust's ownership\n- The C code uses raw pointers everywhere. In Rust, use `Rc<...>` or `Rc<RefCell<...>>` for shared mutable state (e.g., the global `Rec` env, value pointers).\n- It's OK to change struct field types if needed (e.g., make `next: Option<Rc<RefCell<Rec>>>` instead of `Option<Box<Rec>>`).\n- If you change struct fields, make sure other modules still compile.\n- The `Prim = fn(Tsp, Rec, Val) -> Val` signature won't match `&mut Tsp, &mut Rec` — you may want to change `Prim` to a compatible signature (e.g., `fn(&mut Tsp, &Rc<RefCell<Rec>>, Rc<Val>) -> Option<Rc<Val>>`).\n\nWork through this carefully. Don't try to do everything at once — start with the data types, then `tisp_env_init`, `mk_*` functions, then the reader, the evaluator, and the printer. Build/test frequently.\n\nWhen you're done, run `cargo build` and `cargo test` and confirm both succeed. Report the final status.","agentId":"a23a6d252f6b8c9f8","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both `cargo build` and `cargo test` succeed and the `run_tests` test passes all 43 assertions.\n\n## Summary\n\nSuccessfully ported the C tisp interpreter to Rust. Key implementation details:\n\n**Architecture changes from scaffold:**\n- Changed `Prim` signature to `fn(&mut Tsp, &Rc<RefCell<Rec>>, Val) -> Option<Val>` to work with Rust's borrow checker\n- Used `Rc<RefCell<Rec>>` for shared mutable environment records (mirrors C's pointer-sharing semantics)\n- Used `HashMap<String, Val>` inside `Rec` instead of a hand-rolled hashmap with capacity-based linear probing\n- Added `ValUnion::None` variant and `Clone` derive on `Val`/`ValUnion` to allow clean Rust-style ownership\n\n**Functions implemented in `/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-GbuWfb/project/src/tisp.rs`:**\n- Constructors: `mk_val`, `mk_int`, `mk_dec`, `mk_rat` (with `frac_reduce` for